{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1916 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f8f2a91477ab338e308b7637d3c50129d702aef57ccbd9cbacc2676eb1a91c1f" :edb/db-before-t 1915 :edb/db-after-t 1916 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535228 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:00:30.217Z" 13194139535228 true] [17592186221342 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338681 13194139535228 true] [17592186221342 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338688 13194139535228 true] [17592186221343 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338713 13194139535228 true] [17592186221344 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338720 13194139535228 true] [17592186221911 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535228 true] [17592186221911 1100 "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a first-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3}$\n- $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{5}$" 13194139535228 true] [17592186221911 1100 "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a first-order numerical method?\n\n1. $τ(Δx) = 6(Δx)^{3}$\n2. $τ(Δx) = 0.3(Δx)^{2} + 2(Δx)^{3}$\n3. $τ(Δx) = 9(Δx)^{3} + 4(Δx)^{5}$" 13194139535228 false] [17592186221911 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{2}=0.3(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{3}=9(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139535228 true] [17592186338681 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338682 13194139535228 true] [17592186338681 1089 "q-334648" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338681 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535228 true] [17592186338681 1100 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.35,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.35)=0.75.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.005.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." 13194139535228 true] [17592186338681 1113 #uuid "a0158779-3bb5-5926-932e-57d4963e6b20" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338681 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.35$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.35}{0.10}=3.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(3.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.75$ to $0.005.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.75}{0.005}=150.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.5)^{p+1} & ≈150 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3.5}⁡(150) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.999… \\\\ p & ≈2.999….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338683 13194139535228 true] [17592186338682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338684 13194139535228 true] [17592186338682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338685 13194139535228 true] [17592186338682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338686 13194139535228 true] [17592186338682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338687 13194139535228 true] [17592186338682 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535228 true] [17592186338682 1041 "selection" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338682 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338683 13194139535228 true] [17592186338682 1110 #uuid "afaea038-a4d6-5c43-aaaf-06cbe4b5e728" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338683 1034 "3" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338683 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338683 1111 #uuid "371d5d0d-0a4c-515c-b2db-00ad19b0f3f1" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338684 1034 "2" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338684 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338684 1111 #uuid "86b8bfd6-8cdf-5ba6-9701-86a2fc8096ba" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338685 1034 "1" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338685 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338685 1111 #uuid "70b683ac-d889-5751-928a-2e3244869cda" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338686 1034 "4" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338686 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338686 1111 #uuid "304d8479-26b9-532c-8eb4-750823e6c56c" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338687 1034 "5" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338687 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338687 1111 #uuid "b3588f0c-aad1-5915-a6f2-6c86ce681172" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338688 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338689 13194139535228 true] [17592186338688 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338695 13194139535228 true] [17592186338688 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338701 13194139535228 true] [17592186338688 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338707 13194139535228 true] [17592186338688 1089 "q-334710" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338688 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535228 true] [17592186338688 1100 "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}-(Δx)^{6}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.4,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139535228 true] [17592186338688 1113 #uuid "191ac454-645f-542b-be97-450b5f3d1a65" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338688 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.6) & =6(0.6)^{5}-(0.6)^{6} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.077\\,76-0.046\\,656 \\\\  & =0.466\\,56-0.046\\,656 \\\\  & =0.419\\,904 \\\\  & ≈0.420.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.4) & =6(0.4)^{5}-(0.4)^{6} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.010\\,24-0.004\\,096 \\\\  & =0.061\\,44-0.004\\,096 \\\\  & =0.057\\,344 \\\\  & ≈0.057.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.4.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.4}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={1.5}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.419\\,904$ to $0.057\\,344.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.419\\,904}{0.057\\,344}=7.322\\,54…≈7.323.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1.5}^{p+1} & ≈7.322\\,54… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{1.5}⁡(7.322\\,54…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.910… \\\\ p & ≈3.910….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}-(Δx)^{6}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{5})=O\\,((Δx)^{4+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338689 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338690 13194139535228 true] [17592186338689 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338691 13194139535228 true] [17592186338689 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338692 13194139535228 true] [17592186338689 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338693 13194139535228 true] [17592186338689 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338694 13194139535228 true] [17592186338689 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535228 true] [17592186338689 1041 "field-1" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338689 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338691 13194139535228 true] [17592186338689 1110 #uuid "c373e60e-d399-5c20-a016-14da2f05a5c6" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338690 1034 "0.057" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338690 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338690 1111 #uuid "40b938c1-d0a2-56e9-97a0-b28d20fdd30c" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338691 1034 "0.420" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338691 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338691 1111 #uuid "5684bcf7-4718-5eb3-8a21-4853389798ab" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338692 1034 "0.375" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338692 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338692 1111 #uuid "07522058-7c46-5427-85c3-dc577aea7ceb" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338693 1034 "0.075" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338693 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338693 1111 #uuid "08c848bb-653a-533a-85d4-dfa289c846fe" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338694 1034 "0.550" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338694 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338694 1111 #uuid "c054062f-958a-5f8b-8d2c-b7b24a5c2a02" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338695 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338696 13194139535228 true] [17592186338695 10