 #edb/ref 17592186338705 13194139535228 true] [17592186338701 1110 #uuid "300b39fc-7b95-554d-8671-63f747e0950f" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338702 1034 "4.361" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338702 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338702 1111 #uuid "15346b38-82c5-5175-9983-986f19f2b702" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338703 1034 "8.603" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338703 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338703 1111 #uuid "eb190b94-153e-540b-b093-8d0a5facec2b" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338704 1034 "5.957" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338704 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338704 1111 #uuid "f0a001ed-13cd-505f-bd0b-d89cfa4cd562" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338705 1034 "7.323" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338705 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338705 1111 #uuid "95c22070-1d88-5664-bec3-f3ae2645b889" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338706 1034 "10.065" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338706 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338706 1111 #uuid "2b31bbd8-ecb4-5671-bd1d-8fe706d635f8" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338708 13194139535228 true] [17592186338707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338709 13194139535228 true] [17592186338707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338710 13194139535228 true] [17592186338707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338711 13194139535228 true] [17592186338707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338712 13194139535228 true] [17592186338707 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535228 true] [17592186338707 1041 "field-4" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338707 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338711 13194139535228 true] [17592186338707 1110 #uuid "4f04376b-d047-53db-b69d-099eb90dfe96" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338708 1034 "3" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338708 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338708 1111 #uuid "5ac49277-9642-5c6b-9596-69d35d3f7602" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338709 1034 "2" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338709 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338709 1111 #uuid "c4c8fd57-3e21-562a-8bbb-f728f7447f5d" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338710 1034 "1" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338710 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338710 1111 #uuid "158c09d9-e845-5d1d-8f11-89ff8f10efe6" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338711 1034 "4" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338711 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338711 1111 #uuid "7ace811c-6d6b-5be9-9501-bbf7d47faeb1" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338712 1034 "5" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338712 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338712 1111 #uuid "002805ea-0950-5056-aa0b-779c8cfb3fdd" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338713 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338714 13194139535228 true] [17592186338713 1089 "q-334761" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338713 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535228 true] [17592186338713 1100 "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7}$\n- $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5}$" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338713 1113 #uuid "49d42f68-ce62-5114-8630-2000eca229d8" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338713 1145 "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.5(Δx)^{4}=0.5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{2}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535228 true] [17592186338714 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338715 13194139535228 true] [17592186338714 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338716 13194139535228 true] [17592186338714 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338717 13194139535228 true] [17592186338714 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338718 13194139535228 true] [17592186338714 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338719 13194139535228 true] [17592186338714 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535228 true] [17592186338714 1041 "selection" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338714 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338718 13194139535228 true] [17592186338714 1110 #uuid "b8421b7e-5b2a-5070-98d2-12fb201b0d03" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338715 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338715 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535228 true] [17592186338715 1111 #uuid "a49d378a-b07f-5e09-85f6-5fcc96f7fedf" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338716 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338716 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535228 true] [17592186338716 1111 #uuid "287d93c6-0cdf-5acd-b44a-b5d41b24e85c" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338717 1034 "I only" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338717 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535228 true] [17592186338717 1111 #uuid "74e26eb5-2461-52e6-9366-65648ba85ab4" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338718 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338718 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535228 true] [17592186338718 1111 #uuid "8c4ccffc-c54c-54c0-b429-83f623aa78b8" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338719 1034 "II only" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338719 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535228 true] [17592186338719 1111 #uuid "0051b7f1-7fda-58b6-ae60-9ef229dd4508" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338720 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338721 13194139535228 true] [17592186338720 1089 "q-334763" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338720 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535228 true] [17592186338720 1100 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.2$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.2)≈0.064.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338720 1113 #uuid "b4057e0b-c56a-5d6e-a19f-cf1eace4c3c9" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338720 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.2$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.2}{0.1}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}={2}^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.1)≈\\frac{0.064}{8}=0.008.\n$$" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338721 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338722 13194139535228 true] [17592186338721 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535228 true] [17592186338721 1041 "field-1" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338721 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338722 13194139535228 true] [17592186338721 1110 #uuid "8b88f266-70c0-5413-b79d-d7e4231774ff" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338722 1034 "0.008" 13194139535228 true] [17592186338722 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535228 true] [17592186338722 1111 #uuid "0bd72683-d4b7-5963-a523-5ad25dcde25f" 13194139535228 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-334648" 17592186338681 "q-334648/selection" 17592186338682 "q-334648/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f